      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "At least 7 days before baking: combine the dried mixed fruit, currants and raisins in a jar. Pour over the rum, seal and leave at room temperature, shaking daily."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "On baking day: heat the oven to 160°C. Line the rectangular baking tin with a double layer of baking paper, including the sides."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Blend the macerated fruit and any remaining rum in the food processor to a rough paste. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Beat the butter and dark-brown sugar together until pale. Beat in the eggs one at a time. Add the molasses, vanilla and spices. Fold in the flour, then fold in the fruit paste until fully combined."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour into the lined tin and level the top. Bake for 2 hours until a skewer comes out clean. Cool in the tin, then wrap tightly. The cake keeps for weeks."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "The top is dark and cracking before the centre is set.",
              "solution": "Cover the tin with foil after the first hour of baking. The oven may also be running slightly hot; use an oven thermometer to check."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "The cake is very dry.",
              "solution": "This usually means the fruit was not soaked long enough or the bake went over time. Pierce the cooled cake with a skewer and spoon over a few tablespoons of rum to moisten it."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "The fruit sinks to the bottom.",
              "solution": "The batter was too thin. If the macerated fruit retained a lot of rum, the batter becomes very wet. Drain the fruit more thoroughly before blending."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Black cake is the centrepiece of Caribbean Christmas and wedding celebrations. Every island has its own version and family recipes are closely guarded. Jamaicans call it Christmas cake; Trinidadians call it rum cake. It is given as gifts, shipped abroad to family diaspora communities, and eaten in thin slices with ice cream."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
